      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism remains a dominant and mainstream framework in most law school curricula and on the Supreme Court, though it continues to be a subject of significant debate. While some critical legal scholars dismiss it as inherently political or a \"partisan ideology,\" the Souter and Scalia eras and recent opinions show it is deeply embedded in judicial practice. Ultimately, its legitimacy depends less on consensus and more on whether one accepts that historical facts are knowable and that the Constitution should be fixed to its original contours.",
